      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "Orbital Matter 3D-printed the first rigid beam in the vacuum of space",
      "d": "An in-space construction first could be an enabler for the space data centers of the future.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/03/orbital-matter-3d-printed-the-first-rigid-beam-in-the-vacuum-of-space/",
      "m": "11. 03. PM 08:57",
      "ts": 1793739437000,
      "fullText": "A satellite built by the German startup Orbital Matter 3D-printed a half-meter long beam out of a custom plastic material, in what executives called a first for a free-flying spacecraft in orbit, and a step toward constructing large structures in space. The company&#8217;s small satellite, Replicator 2, was launched on a SpaceX rideshare mission this summer, with four of the company&#8217;s 3D printers onboard. Each printer can create a rigid, cylindrical beam by extruding coils of resin that are cured by an ultraviolet light, which the company analogizes to cement and water. Loading the player&#8230; This technology matters because future spacecraft will need to be larger in order to generate more electrical power and radiate a more heat into the vacuum of space, particularly if they&#8217;re going to serve as orbital data centers. But they&#8217;ll still need to fit into the tight confines of the fairing on top of the rocket that carries them into orbit. If spacecraft are able to construct their own structures in orbit, the power and heat-dissipation challenges may get a lot easier to solve. &#8220;Replicator 2 is the first proof that we can simply build the array in space, at whatever size the mission demands,” said Jakub Stojek, CEO and co-founder of Orbital Matter. The company is partnering with the European Space Agency and Thales Alenia, a leading European satellite builder, with which it collaborated on Replicator 2. Today&#8217;s large structures in space are origami projects — large solar arrays or telescope mirrors are folded up and then deployed on orbit. That&#8217;s a tricky engineering project, and so the idea of making and assembling those structures in space is appealing. NASA has funded some efforts at the idea, notably a mission with the company Redwire that 3D-printed structural beams in a vacuum chamber in 2023 but never got off the ground. Two of Orbital Matter&#8217;s printers are designed to deploy a flexible solar array, but an anomaly led o"
